WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.734
Axum applications need consistent error
behavior because every error path is still

2
00:00:08.814 --> 00:00:11.713
part of the public interface.

3
00:00:11.793 --> 00:00:19.100
Validation failures, authentication
failures, authorization denials,

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.862
missing resources, upstream failures,
database errors,

5
00:00:24.942 --> 00:00:32.828
and unexpected internal problems should be
mapped to responses intentionally.

6
00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:41.258
The client should understand the outcome
without receiving details that belong only

7
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:43.890
in internal diagnostics.

8
00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:51.044
User-facing responses should avoid
secrets, raw tokens, stack traces,

9
00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:57.619
database details, internal topology,
sensitive configuration,

10
00:00:57.699 --> 00:01:02.569
and implementation-specific failure
messages.

11
00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:09.260
A production service can be transparent
about success, rejection,

12
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:15.255
and retry behavior without exposing how
the system is built.

13
00:01:15.335 --> 00:01:23.916
Safe response design reduces unnecessary
information disclosure while still helping

14
00:01:23.996 --> 00:01:28.519
legitimate clients handle errors
correctly.

15
00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:33.586
Logging and response content should be
separated.

16
00:01:33.666 --> 00:01:40.161
Operators may need correlation IDs, route
names, status classes,

17
00:01:40.241 --> 00:01:47.431
dependency names, latency, and sanitized
context to diagnose a problem.

18
00:01:47.511 --> 00:01:54.701
Clients usually need a stable status code,
a predictable response shape,

19
00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:58.144
and perhaps a safe error category.

20
00:01:58.224 --> 00:02:06.575
The service should not solve operational
visibility by dumping internal detail into

21
00:02:06.655 --> 00:02:08.510
the HTTP response.

22
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:13.693
Panics should not be treated as normal
control flow.

23
00:02:13.773 --> 00:02:20.963
Rust makes it possible to write robust
error types and explicit mappings,

24
00:02:21.043 --> 00:02:27.190
and Axum can return structured responses
from those mappings.

25
00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.749
Unexpected panics may still occur,

26
00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:38.831
but production design should define how
they are contained, logged, correlated,

27
00:02:38.911 --> 00:02:40.419
and monitored.

28
00:02:40.499 --> 00:02:46.645
A panic is an operational signal, not a
normal validation path.

29
00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:51.364
Consistency makes error behavior
reviewable.

30
00:02:51.444 --> 00:02:58.866
Similar failures should produce similar
status codes and response shapes.

31
00:02:58.946 --> 00:03:05.557
Sensitive routes should not leak more
detail than ordinary routes.

32
00:03:05.637 --> 00:03:12.131
Errors should be redacted before logs
leave the service boundary.

33
00:03:12.211 --> 00:03:18.010
The goal is a system where developers,
security reviewers,

34
00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:25.860
and operators can reason about both client
behavior and incident diagnostics.
